(1) Proposition 5.2(b): TV(Q_F, Q_P0) = sum_x mu(x)(1 - P0(F(x)|x)) >= 1 - sum_x mu(x) max_y P0(y|x), exact, 300 random instances: True
    Proposition 5.2(a), all functions F enumerated (u in {7, 10, 12}, uniform and non-uniform mu, coin and other samplers, 1 or 12 weighted tests):
    E_F Q_F(R) = Q_P0(R) exactly: True; max Pr_F[some test deviates by more than the bound]/eta = 0.2431 (must be <= 1); largest deviation/bound seen = 2.026
    consequence: (delta/2) e^(2N) / 2^(3N) < 2^(-N/10) for all N < 400 and delta <= 1: True; e^2/8 = 0.92363 < 2^(-1/10) = 0.93303
    u = 4096, N = 1024, pi = 1/1490, delta = 0.1: bound of (a) = 0.0355 = sqrt(N/u) tol_N = 0.0355; tol_N = 0.0709
(2) Example 5.3, u = 8, all 256 functions g, 40 random per-call tests: the formulas for a(B) and h(B) agree with the definitions of Section 6.1: True;
    'the two answers agree' has probability 1 for every g and 1/2 for the human system: True by the same enumeration
(3) Theorem 6.1 on 60 random (stateful answerer, human) pairs, scaffold with runs of 1 or 2 calls, exact multinomial sums over m <= 7 rounds:
    the means of alpha_i(B), eta_i(B) equal a(B), h(B) of the definition: True; max failure/delta of (a) = 0.0000, of (b) = 0.1997 (must be <= 1);
    probability that (b) holds and one of its two stated consequences fails: 0.0e+00 (must be 0)
    tolerances: paired form sqrt(2 ln(2/(pi delta))/m) is exactly twice sqrt(ln(2/(pi delta))/(2m)): True
(4) Theorem 6.2 on 120 random adaptive procedures (4 inputs, 2 or 3 answers, non-uniform P0, m = 3, internal randomness), all functions enumerated:
    (c) |E_F Pr_F(accept) - Pr_P0(accept)| <= xi: True (largest gap 0.2762); equality when no question is repeated: True;
    (d) Pr[F in G] >= 1 - eta and some f in G has Pr_f(accept) >= Pr_P0(accept) - xi - eta: True
    the procedure 'ask input 0, accept iff the answer is 0' (no repetition, xi = 0): Pr_coin(accept) = 1/2, and |Pr_f(accept) - 1/2| takes the values [0.5] over all f;
    so (d) cannot be strengthened to a two-sided statement for one f: only the average over F is exactly indistinguishable (statement (c))
    run laws of the coin and of a function f for adaptive scaffolds whose runs make L = 3 calls: TV = 1 - 2^-L exactly in 50 random scaffolds: True
(5) Example 6.3, d = 1: all 4 tests (with repetition over the ignored coordinate) that ignore one input bit have equal frequencies for the parity and the coin: True; the test 'y = parity(x)' has frequency 1 against 1/2
(5) Example 6.3, d = 2: all 32 tests (with repetition over the ignored coordinate) that ignore one input bit have equal frequencies for the parity and the coin: True; the test 'y = parity(x)' has frequency 1 against 1/2
(5) Example 6.3, d = 3: all 768 tests (with repetition over the ignored coordinate) that ignore one input bit have equal frequencies for the parity and the coin: True; the test 'y = parity(x)' has frequency 1 against 1/2
